  "culture": {
    "title": "Dogs, and the city after dark",
    "md": "Athens was full of dogs. Every house of any size kept one at the door, and vase painters put them under the dining couches, beside boys on their way to school and at the feet of the dead on gravestones. Hunting hounds from Laconia were the aristocrat's pride (Xenophon wrote a whole treatise on them), heavy Molossians guarded farms and flocks, and tiny long-haired Maltese dogs sat on ladies' laps. A dog's name usually described it: Labros means 'boisterous, greedy'. In Aristophanes' *Wasps* (422 BC) a household dog called Labes, 'Grabber', is put on trial for stealing a Sicilian cheese; his accuser is another dog. The Athenians laughed because they knew the type.\n\nAfter sunset the city went dark. There was no street lighting; anyone who had to go out carried a torch or a small clay oil lamp (λύχνος), and most people simply stayed in. The streets of Kydathenaion were narrow, crooked and unpaved, easy to lose a dog in and easy to lose yourself in. Public order, such as it was, rested with the magistrates and their three hundred Scythian archers, publicly owned slaves who did the rough work of policing; there were no patrols. Syros sleeping at the workshop is not unusual: slaves often lived where they worked, and a kiln that had been fired that day kept the place warm and dry. Dogs knew that too.",
    "image": "culture-dog-wasps"
  },
